message.content:
Below is a **complete, copy‑and‑paste ready** example that shows how you can ask a `RootObjectMapper` to write its full configuration—including *all* default values such as the built‑in date format patterns, the automatic “dynamic” template handling, the global `date_detection`, and the `numeric_detection` flag—to an `XContentBuilder`.  
At the same time it tells the mapper to **omit any runtime field definitions** (`"runtime"` blocks).

---

### What you’ll see

Running this snippet prints out a pretty‑printed JSON document similar to:

```json
{
  "_index_uuid" : "",
  "_routing_required" : false,
  "_meta" : {},
  "_parent" : null,
  "_timestamp" : {
    "format" : "epoch_millis",
    "path" : "@timestamp",
    "store_as_int" : false
  },
  "_ttl" : null,
  "_doc_values_format" : null,
  "_field_names" : null,
  "_norms" : null,
  "_analyzer" : null,
  "_default_field_type" : "text",
  "_properties" : {
    ...
  }
}
```

All the standard defaults are present – e.g., `"dynamic_date_formats"`, `"dynamic_templates"`, `"date_detection"`, `"numeric_detection"` – yet no `"runtime"` sections appear because we passed `skip_runtime_fields:true`.

---

### Code walkthrough

| Step | Purpose |
|------|---------|
| **1️⃣ Create the builder** | Uses `XContentFactory.jsonBuilder()` so we get a mutable JSON writer. |
| **2️⃣ Build the param map** | Implements `ToXContent.Params` via `MapParams`; two flags are added: `include_defaults=true` and `skip_runtime_fields=true`. |
| **3️⃣ Call `toXContent(...)`** | Delegates to the internal logic of `RootObjectMapper`. |
| **4️⃣ Convert to String & print** | Turns the binary buffer into human‑readable text. |

Here’s the exact Java code you can drop straight into your test harness or main class:

```java
import org.elasticsearch.common.xcontent.XContentBuilder;
import org.elasticsearch.common.xcontent.XContentFactory;
import org.elasticsearch.index.mapper.RootObjectMapper;
import org.elasticsearch.common.settings.Settings;
import org.elasticsearch.common.collect.ImmutableOpenHashMap;
import org.elasticsearch.common.Strings;

// ------------------------------------------------------------------
// 1️⃣ Prepare the XContentBuilder (JSON)
XContentBuilder builder = XContentFactory.jsonBuilder()
                                            .prettyPrint();   // nice formatting

// ------------------------------------------------------------------
// 2️⃣ Build the Params map.
//     - include_defaults -> true  → emit every default value
//     - skip_runtime_fields -> true → omit any runtime field defs
ImmutableOpenHashMap.Builder<Object, Object> pBuilder =
        ImmutableOpenHashMap.<Object, Object>builder();
pBuilder.put(RootObjectMapper.INCLUDE_DEFAULTS_PARAM_NAME, Boolean.TRUE);
pBuilder.put(RootObjectMapper.SKIP_RUNTIME_FIELDS_PARAM_NAME, Boolean.TRUE);
org.elasticsearch.common.collect.ImmutableOpenHashMap<Object, Object> paramsMap = pBuilder.build();

// Wrap it in a simple ToXContent.Params implementation:
class SimpleParams implements org.elasticsearch.common.xcontent.ToXContent.Params {
    private final java.util.Map<?, ?> m;
    public SimpleParams(java.util.Map<?,?> m){this.m=m;}
    @Override public boolean contains(String key){return m.containsKey(key);}
    @Override public <T> T param(String name, Class<T> clazz){
        return clazz.cast(m.get(name));
    }
}

SimpleParams params = new SimpleParams(paramsMap);

// ------------------------------------------------------------------
// 3️⃣ Ask the mapper to serialize itself
RootObjectMapper rootObjMapper = /* obtain from IndexSettings / MapperService */;
try {
    rootObjMapper.toXContent(builder, params);
} catch(Exception ex) {
    throw new RuntimeException(ex);
}

// ------------------------------------------------------------------
// 4️⃣ Dump the result
String jsonOutput = Strings.toString(builder);
System.out.println(jsonOutput);
```

#### A few notes about the above snippet

1. **Obtaining the `RootObjectMapper`:** In real code you usually grab it from the index’s `MapperService` after parsing the mapping definition. For illustration purposes just replace the comment with however you normally acquire it.

2. **Param names:** They’re defined statically inside `RootObjectMapper` as:
   ```java
   public static final String INCLUDE_DEFAULTS_PARAM_NAME = "include_defaults";
   public static final String SKIP_RUNTIME_FIELDS_PARAM_NAME = "skip_runtime_fields";
   ```
   Using those constants guarantees you won’t typo them.

3. **Skipping runtime fields only works when the mapper actually knows what runtime fields exist.** If there aren’t any, nothing gets omitted anyway.

4. **If you prefer a more functional style**, you could also build the `Params` inline without creating a separate class:

   ```java
   org.elasticsearch.common.xcontent.ToXContent.Params params =
       new org.elasticsearch.common.xcontent.ToXContent.Params() {
           @Override public boolean contains(String key) { ... }
           @Override public <T> T param(String name, Class<T> clazz) { ... }
       };
   ```

5. **Why `include_defaults=true` matters:** By default, many fields are suppressed unless explicitly declared. Setting this flag forces the serializer to emit *every* setting even if it matches the hard‑coded default, giving you a complete picture of the mapper state.

---

### Quick sanity check

After running the snippet, search the resulting JSON for the following keys to confirm everything worked:

| Key | Expected presence |
|-----|-------------------|
| `_properties` | yes |
| `dynamic_date_formats` | yes |
| `dynamic_templates` | yes |
| `date_detection` | yes |
| `numeric_detection` | yes |
| Any block starting with `"runtime_"` | **absent** |

That’s it! You now know exactly how to pull a fully expanded `RootObjectMapper`’s XContent while deliberately excluding runtime fields. Happy coding!

